 "open_findings": [
  {
   "id": "F1",
   "found": "by the implementer in the fix round, while measuring what the archive reader still holds in memory",
   "status": "ruled",
   "finding": "A tar name or a pax value longer than 536,870,888 UTF-16 units (the longest string of the JavaScript runtime) ends the port in a RangeError of the runtime, where the oracle accepts the archive. Measured with a pax value and a GNU long name of 600 MiB: the oracle accepts at 2.4 GiB and 1.8 GiB peak, the port fails closed at 1.2 GiB. At 300 MiB both accept. Present at ac5e89c as well; the fix round did not change it.",
   "asks": "Ruled in the second fix round: a declared refusal, divergence D3.",
   "backlog": "B8",
   "ruling": "D3"
  },
  {
   "id": "F4",
   "found": "by the implementer in the second fix round, while checking that the limit of D3 is enough on its own",
   "status": "not ruled",
   "finding": "Valid archives at or below the metadata limit of D3 on which the oracle answers and the port does not: the port runs out of heap and the process aborts (\"JavaScript heap out of memory\", exit by SIGABRT, no receipt). The heap limit is the runtime's, about 4 GiB by default on Node 22.23.3 and 24.14.0, less on a small machine, more with --max-old-space-size; it can come before the memory of the machine does. The oracle has no such ceiling: it ends in an uncaught MemoryError only when the machine has no more. It fails closed. No limit on a single block removes it.",
   "measured": [
    "One pax header of 268,435,417 bytes (below the limit) that holds one GNU.sparse.map of 2**27 numbers: the oracle accepts in 9 s at 7.9 GiB peak; the port aborts after 29 s at 6.0 GiB resident.",
    "Sixteen members, each with a GNU long name of exactly 2**28 bytes (4 GiB of names): the oracle accepts in 5 s at 4.6 GiB peak; the port aborts after 31 s at 4.4 GiB resident. With eight such members both accept (oracle 2.6 GiB, port 4.2 GiB).",
    "Read by both engines at the limit: 53.6 million pax records of five bytes (oracle 27 s, port 7 s); 2**24 + 4,096 distinct pax keywords (oracle 8 s at 2.2 GiB, port 3 s at 0.3 GiB, after the fix F3); 5.9 million records of the sparse form 0.0 (oracle 7 s, port 4 s)."
   ],
   "asks": "Rule whether this stays a stated memory limit of the runtime, or becomes a declared refusal (for example a budget on the metadata an archive may make the reader keep). The manager of the dispatch chose to leave it open and blocking, and takes it to the technical owner.",
   "backlog": "B11"
  }
 ],
 "backlog": [
  {
   "id": "B1",
   "for": "oracle",
   "entry": "Refuse a recipe that is not UTF-8 in `verify_bytes`, in both engines, by a reviewed waiver."
  },
  {
   "id": "B2",
   "for": "oracle and plan",
   "entry": "Bound document depth explicitly, well below the interpreter limits, so that no refusal depends on the caller's depth."
  },
  {
   "id": "B3",
   "for": "plan",
   "entry": "Pin the oracle interpreter by the sha256 of its files (urllib/parse.py, tarfile.py, gzip.py, json/decoder.py), not by the name \"CPython 3.12.3\"."
  },
  {
   "id": "B4",
   "for": "oracle",
   "entry": "Refuse an archive whose tar listing ends on an invalid header instead of an end marker."
  },
  {
   "id": "B5",
   "for": "port, when cook and catalog are ported",
   "entry": "Decide whether the key order of the merged configuration is observable downstream. If it is, port the order rule of `merge_hash` (an equal upper mapping replaces the lower one)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B6",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "`combine` in src/config.ts is the stand-in and matches Ansible only in the `merge_layers` shape. A second caller needs Ansible's semantics for three or more layers and for layers that are not mappings."
  },
  {
   "id": "B7",
   "for": "port, with cook or deploy",
   "entry": "The end-to-end witness for archive.L140.4 (obligation above)."
  },
  {
   "id": "B8",
   "for": "closed",
   "entry": "Open finding F1, ruled as divergence D3 in the second fix round."
  },
  {
   "id": "B9",
   "for": "port",
   "entry": "The stepwise gzip reader drives Node's zlib engine through the call `inflateRawSync` uses internally, on the Node versions of STEPWISE_RUNTIMES only (ruling B9). Replace it when Node documents a synchronous inflate that can be resumed. Until then two modes of a changed engine end as a crash and not as `UnsupportedRuntime` (review finding L2): an engine whose errors arrive only asynchronously leaves an unhandled `error` event, and a change the checks of the reader do not name surfaces as whatever the runtime throws."
  },
  {
   "id": "B10",
   "for": "port, with the command line (plan phase 7)",
   "entry": "Check the runtime once at start and report an unverified one as a missing prerequisite with a public code, before any archive is read. Today it surfaces at the first archive, as `UnsupportedRuntime` without a code."
  },
  {
   "id": "B11",
   "for": "ruling",
   "entry": "Open finding F4: archives at or below the metadata limit that exhaust the heap of the runtime."
  }
 ],
 "fixed_without_ruling": [
  {
   "id": "F2",
   "found": "by the implementer in the second fix round, while checking that the limit of D3 is enough on its own",
   "finding": "Two patterns of the tar reader ended in `+$` and took time quadratic in a run that is not at the end: `rstrip(\"/\")` of a pax path and the blanks around a pax number. Measured at a198042: 6 to 8 s for a run of 160,000 characters, so hours for a few MiB, where the oracle takes milliseconds. Present at ac5e89c as well. No wrong answer, no answer.",
   "fix": "Both are one pass now (`rstripSlash`, `DecimalScanner`), and no more than 4,300 digits of a number are kept. The helper differential compares `int()` on 9,688 generated texts and the tar listing on 12,000 archives; test/unit/port-tar-limits.test.js bounds the time on runs of 2 MiB."
  },
  {
   "id": "F3",
   "found": "as F2",
   "finding": "A pax header with very many records ended the port where the oracle accepts the archive. The port kept every record of every header: two Buffers in a list and an entry in a Map, as the oracle keeps them in a dict; global headers accumulate over the archive. Measured with one header of 2**24 + 4,096 distinct keywords (185 MB of records, below the limit of D3): the oracle accepts in 8 s at 2.2 GiB; the build of a198042 aborts with \"JavaScript heap out of memory\" at 4.7 GiB. A Map of the runtime also ends at 2**24 keys (\"RangeError: Map maximum size exceeded\"), which that build would have met next. Present at ac5e89c as well.",
   "fix": "Only the nine keywords the reader looks at are kept, in the order and with the values the dict would give them; no other record can reach a name, a size or an exception. The values of the two keywords the sparse form 0.0 reads from the raw records are kept as byte ranges. The same header now: accepted in 3 s at 0.3 GiB."
  }
 ]
}
